Abstract

This paper aims to demonstrate that ecological interface design reduces the use of cognitive resources. Making the boundaries of accep t able performance visually perceivable should elicit skill-based behavior, thus lowering the cognitive load. To illustrate the psychological mechanisms of ecological interface design and validate its influence on cognitive load, we compared a conventional speedometer to an ecological speedometer. Both interfaces were displayed on the head-up display of a static driving simulator and were tested in a rural highway scenario. In a 2 × 2 × 2 repeated-measure design, the human–machine interface was tested along with the informative intelligent speed assistance system (representing an existing alternative for speed control) and the additional workload induced by a 1-back task (representing cognitively demanding driving situations). To measure cognitive load, we used a tactile version of the detection response task and controlled for all confounding variables. The experiment was conducted with 28 male and 21 female drivers. Our hypotheses and the ecological interface design theory are supported by the finding that the ecological speedometer reduces the use of cognitive resources. Moreover, the specific application of the ecological speedometer enables better driving performance (i.e., longitudinal and lateral control) compared to a conventional speedometer.
Introduction

Advances in hardware and software provide engineers with the possibility to design interfaces that can provide drivers with a substantial amount of information and entertainment. However, increased input might also increase the driver’s cognitive load, which is defined as the demand for cognitive control (Engström et al., 2013, International Organization for Standardization, 2016). From a more neuroscientific perspective, automatized behavior relies on strong neural pathways trained on perceptual-motor contingencies with valuable and consistent outcomes, whereas nonautomatized behavior builds on weaker pathways caused by varied and relatively infrequent contingencies (Cohen et al., 1990, Miller and Cohen, 2001, Shiffrin and Schneider, 1977). Following the cognitive control hypothesis proposed by Engström, Markkula, Victor, and Merat (2017), cognitive control is needed to flexibly boost these weaker neural pathways to allow for nonautomatized and goal-directed behavior. Therefore, cognitive resources that are demanded by any nonautomatized secondary task specifically impair tasks that are also nonautomatized and thus share the demand for cognitive control. However, because cognitive control is not needed, automatized performance is unaffected by the induced cognitive load.

Whenever the cognitive load becomes too high, weaker pathways can no longer be flexibly boosted, and the stronger pathways serve as a kind of fallback level. For example, cognitively loaded drivers rely on this fallback level and would most likely brake sufficiently before an obstacle because looming effects automatically trigger a braking reaction (Franconeri & Simons, 2003). However, cognitively loaded drivers might not be able to properly adapt to predictive cues, such as road signs and roadside instructions (Baumann et al., 2008, Engström and Markkula, 2007) or dangerous road conditions (Cooper et al., 2003). Moreover, capture errors, as described by Norman (1981), might happen more often to cognitively loaded drivers, when a partially familiar situation unconsciously activates an inappropriate automatized behavior. Thus, if the automatized behavioral patterns are sufficient to handle a given situation, cognitive load generates no safety hazard. However, whenever the automatized behavior cannot handle a given situation properly, severe accidents might occur (Engström et al., 2017).

Therefore, to save on scarce cognitive resources and prohibit possibly dangerous automatized behavior, the literature suggests ecological interface design as an effective framework for designing interfaces in a way that theoretically minimizes the cognitive load (Vicente, 2002). More specifically, ecological interface design is an approach that seeks to display information about the constraints and complex relationships of the work environment in a manner that enables information to be directly perceived and easily understood (Hajdukiewicz & Burns, 2004).

A good representation of a system simultaneously visualizes the relevant functions, goal states, current states, functional consequences of time, and critical boundaries for action (Vicente, 1999). In Rasmussen (1983) framework of skills, rules, and knowledge (SRK), ecological interfaces enable a direct shortcut from “activation” to “execution” because they provide consistent perception–action mappings and thus can be used as signals (Fig. 1). This indicates that the information shown by the ecological interface should lead to the automated execution of a certain behavior, without having to first identify and interpret the information and then decide on the appropriate task in the particular situation. Thus, ecological interfaces are aimed at fostering skill-based behavior, which is the most automated and least conscious type of behavior. This theoretically reduces cognitive load, which then can be used to handle other tasks that demand higher cognitive processes.

Following Rasmussen’s model, sensory input has the characteristics of either signals, signs, or symbols. Signals simply represent time–space variables of a spatial and dynamic environment. They allow for direct perception–action coupling, which is unconscious and generates no cognitive load. In contrast, signs are features of the environment that refer to learned rules. Signs cannot be processed subconsciously because they are meant to activate rule-based behavior that can be verbalized. Lastly, symbols represent further information that must be formally processed to evaluate an existing problem in a given situation. This kind of behavior is the most conscious behavior and called knowledge-based behavior.

Commonly, an abstraction hierarchy is used to determine which kinds of information should be displayed on a system’s ecological interface (e.g., Jenkins, Stanton, & Walker, 2017). Then, knowing which information is relevant in a certain domain, how it should be presented is determined using the SRK taxonomy from Rasmussen. For example, as discussed by Borst, Suijkerbuijk, Mulder, and Van Paassen (2006), an ecological interface should reveal the affordances of the work domain in a way that supports all three levels of cognitive control. Therefore, the interface should provide signals, signs, and symbols.

To demonstrate the approach of the ecological interface design, we decided to use the example of a human–machine interface (HMI) for speed control. Because speed control is vital for road safety (Elvik et al., 2004, Kloeden et al., 2001, Kröyer, 2015) and advances in infotainment and in-vehicle information systems have increased the cognitive load of drivers (Engström, Johansson, & Östlund, 2005), conventional speedometers may need to be replaced by ecological speedometers. Regarding speed control, no work domain analysis needs to be conducted because it is a basic task that can be executed on different cognitive control levels, either automated or nonautomated. For speed control, both control and set values should be presented.

We consider speed control that occurs while driving a vehicle on a rural highway. While maintaining a certain speed relies on signals, such as optical flow, the driver must use the speedometer to adapt to new speed limits. The usual speedometer presents the current speed as a number that must be cognitively compared to the speed presented on road signs or the HMI of the vehicle. If the current speed is higher, the driver must reduce speed. If it is lower, he or she may increase the speed. In this case, the speedometer is used as a sign, causing nonautomated behavior. Theoretically, such a conventional speedometer cannot be used as a signal because it does not spatially and consistently represent the necessary changes in speed to adjust to a certain limit. Although maintaining a certain speed (stabilization) becomes skill-based after some training, the artificial adaption to certain speed limits (guidance) remains rule based (Donges, 1999). This idea is also in line with the cognitive control hypothesis (Engström et al., 2017), where cognitive control is needed to use variable mapped cues, such as speed signs, whereas consistently mapped perceptual-motor contingencies enable automated behavior. For example, maintaining lanes is based on optical flow and the perceivable boundaries of the street and thus seen to be automatized. As Engström et al. (2017) suggested, even general stimulus–response contingencies may allow for the development of specific automaticity. For example, the ability to unconsciously keep a certain distance to a preceding car (car-following based on optical parameters), may be based on general contingencies learned from walking. Regarding speed control, Recarte and Nunes (2002) argued that every driver’s specific set of automatized behaviors determines a certain speed for a given task demand. However, if no other consistent stimuli exist (e.g., given by an ecological interface), conscious deviations from this individual optimum still rely on cognitive control and contribute to the cognitive load. Under cognitive load, drivers likely return to their automatized behavioral patterns. They devote a major share of cognitive control to the secondary task while unconsciously following their automatized optimum speed, thus ignoring speed signs. This mechanism might also be the reason for unintentional speeding (Lewis-Evans et al., 2011, Recarte and Nunes, 2002).

To design an interface that provides consistent stimulus–response contingencies and therefore can be safely used in a skill-based automated manner, Schewe and Vollrath (2019) used the SRK taxonomy to develop an ecological interface for speed control. A simulated head-up display was used to augment a blue carpet and a bracket in front of the vehicle. The current speed was used to adjust the length of the carpet. The appropriate speed (speed limit) was shown as the augmented bracket. As Fig. 2 illustrates, the carpet ended before the bracket if the driver was driving too slowly (left). It extended over the bracket if the driver was driving too fast (right), and it matched the bracket when the speed was suitable (middle).

Fig. 3 explains how the conventional and ecological distance speedometer communicate on the three different levels of the SRK taxonomy (Rasmussen, 1983). Both HMIs present information as symbols and signs, but the ecological interface can also communicate in the form of signals because it spatially visualizes the speed changes required by the driver. Preexisting stimulus–response contingencies, learned through car-following and other driving situations, are likely to support the automatized processing of the distance speedometer‘s signals.

Schewe and Vollrath (2019) demonstrated that the ecological distance speedometer is well suited for speed control. When reacting to speed signs, the longitudinal driving performance was comparably precise for both the conventional and ecological interface. However, the visual fixation time on the conventional speedometer was higher in comparison to the fixation time on the ecological distance speedometer. Moreover, when reacting to the speed changes of a vehicle ahead, the variance of the speed deviations was significantly lower with the help of the ecological distance speedometer. Nonetheless, this study was unable to determine whether the ecological interface was effective because it enabled skill-based behavior or simply because it displayed the relevant information better by continuously providing direct information about the appropriate speed. If the ecological interface enables skill-based behavior, the speed control should happen unconsciously and thus generate less cognitive load compared to more conscious rule-based behavior generated by the conventional speedometer.

To compare the cognitive load generated by the conventional speedometer or the ecological speedometer, both interfaces were shown in the head-up display. To experimentally vary the requirements of the driving situations, additional cognitive load was artificially induced using the 1-back task (Mehler, Reimer, Coughlin, & Dusek, 2009). Additionally, to allow for a comparison with an existing speeding countermeasure, an informative intelligent speed assistance (ISA) was tested as well. When activated, it issued a warning if the rate of speed was 5 km/h or more too fast. According to Brookhuis and de Waard (1999), the perceived level of the cognitive load does not change when using an ISA system. However, theoretically, such a warning still requires the skill-based control of one’s own speed with the help of a conventional speedometer. It just reminds the driver to devote cognitive control to his or her speed control. In summary, the presented setup was designed to test whether the ecological interface reduces the cognitive load in situations with and without an ISA system and in situations in which additional cognitive load was induced.

To measure the attentional effects of cognitive load, a tactile version of the detection response task (DRT) was used, as described in the ISO Standard 17488 (2016). The task is an indirect measure of cognitive load in the sense that better performance in the DRT indicates that more resources could be used for this task, which indicates that the primary task generates less cognitive load and control demand. This follows the idea of limited cognitive resources according to Wickens’ resource theory (2002) combined with the theory that a specific resource for cognitive control is necessary to handle nonautomatized tasks (Nilsson, Aust, Engström, Svanberg, & Lindén, 2018).

In the following paragraphs, we summarize the conceptual framework for understanding driver attention and cognitive load, as presented in the ISO Standard 17488 (2016). Driver attention can be conceptualized as the bottom-up and top-down allocation of resources to certain activities. These resources, which are allocated to certain activities, can be divided into the following three tiers: sensory/actuator resources, perceptual/motor resources, and cognitive control (on top). Transferred to the comparison between a conventional speedometer and an ecological distance speedometer, the allocation of resources should theoretically appear as they are depicted in Fig. 4.

The figure visualizes resources as boxes that are filled according to the demand for the resource. The differences might not be large because speed control is an easy task, but the effects should nevertheless be detectable and stable, if the theory is correct. Both perceptual and sensory resources and motor and actuator resources should be used in an equal manner by both interfaces. Both HMIs are visually perceived, and both trigger the same kind of manual responses. However, as the information processing framework of the SRK model suggests, cognitive resources should be used less when skill-based and, thus, automatized behavior is enabled and the human uses the given information as a signal instead of being forced to use it as a sign. Therefore, driving using the ecological interface should generate a cognitive load that is comparably low.

Because the HMIs used in this study influence the driving task and theoretically use more or less cognitive resources, it should be possible to measure these differences by the degree to which resources are allocated to the DRT. This idea is also explained in Fig. 5, where the variables are kept constant and the only variation is between the conventional speedometer and ecological distance speedometer. A less demanding HMI should allow for more cognitive control to be allocated to the DRT.

Based on this DRT, the paper should validate that the ecological distance speedometer reduces cognitive load. Moreover, this ecological interface is also expected to improve the control over the vehicle by using an ecological display that elicits the appropriate behavior without conscious processing.

Accordingly, two research questions are addressed in this experiment. Does the ecological interface, following the previous results by Schewe and Vollrath (2019), enhance driving performance? Moreover, does the ecological interface reduce the cognitive load?
